#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
apply-nodalis-v6-fixes.py

===============================================================================
 HOW TO RUN IT
===============================================================================

   1. Put this file in the same folder as your index.html
   2. Open a terminal in that folder
   3. Run:      python3 apply-nodalis-v6-fixes.py index.html
      Windows:  py apply-nodalis-v6-fixes.py index.html

 Your original is kept as index.html.bak
 To undo: delete index.html, rename index.html.bak back to index.html

 It must be run on the file produced by apply-nodalis-v5-fixes.py. If it is
 not, or if it has already been run, it writes NOTHING and says so.

===============================================================================
 THE SIX BUGS
===============================================================================

 1. THE LINKED FOLDER NEEDED RE-LINKING ON EVERY LAUNCH
    Found it. restore() called the silent permission check before setting
    vault.mode, and that check starts with `if (vault.mode !== 'folder') return
    false` - so it returned false without ever asking the browser. Restore
    therefore reported "permission needed" every single time, even when the
    grant had survived. Fixed, plus: when the grant HAS genuinely expired, the
    first tap on the page reconnects and catches up, instead of leaving a chip
    to hunt for in Settings.

 2. "CHANGES NOT BACKED UP" WITH NOTHING CHANGED
    Three causes. A successful push ended by setting githubDirty = false, and
    writing any setting emits settings:changed, which was on the list of events
    that set githubDirty = true - so it turned itself back on three seconds
    after every backup. Theme changes and the start-up activity row did the
    same. And the boot-time pull emitted note:updated per file, so reading from
    disk counted as writing. Now the question is answered by comparing content
    hashes against the manifest push() already builds, and the message tells
    you how many notes actually changed.

 3. A FLASH OF THE EMPTY APP BEFORE THE LOADING SCREEN
    The shell is real HTML, so it painted before any script ran. A curtain in
    <head> now holds it back until the loader is up, and the last-used theme is
    mirrored to localStorage so the first paint is already the right colour.
    A six-second safety timer lifts the curtain even if the app fails to start.

 4. BOTTOM SHEETS DID NOT FOLLOW YOUR FINGER
    The gesture code existed and was correct, but both listeners were passive
    and no touch-action was set - so the browser claimed the gesture as a
    scroll and cancelled it. Now: touch-action on the grip and header, a
    non-passive move handler that calls preventDefault, and pointer capture so
    a fast flick keeps tracking after your finger leaves the sheet. The scrim
    fades with the drag and it springs back if you do not commit.

 5. THE MORE MENU OPENED IN THE MIDDLE OF THE SCREEN
    No anchor was passed, so the menu fell through to its centre-of-viewport
    case. It opens under its own button now.

 6. OVERLAPPING TEXT IN THE "ANOTHER DEVICE GOT THERE FIRST" DIALOG
    That dialog built its own row markup instead of using the shared sheet-row
    classes, so it missed their line-height. On the monospace themes the label
    printed on top of its own description. One implementation now.

===============================================================================
 THE SHARED READER  (the big one)
===============================================================================

 A published note used to be a link to github.com. Now the link opens the note
 IN NODALIS, read-only:

     https://your-site/#/share/<id>

   * the app's own markdown rendering and all six themes
   * the Nodalis name, an install button, and a "make notes of my own" CTA
   * the reader picks their own theme and light/dark - stored only on their
     device, never synced, nothing to do with your settings
   * an outline and a reading-progress bar on longer notes
   * it refreshes when the tab regains focus, so your edits reach them; if the
     text changed while they were reading it is announced rather than swapped
     out mid-sentence
   * nothing is editable, and no app dialogs, tours or install cards appear
   * your notes repository stays private - publishing one note never exposes
     the vault

 Guest mode runs INSTEAD of the app's boot, so no database is opened and no
 feature module starts. A reader cannot reach anyone's notes because in guest
 mode the notes layer is never built.

 Also new: "Keep it updated automatically" republishes a few seconds after
 each edit, and "See what the reader sees" opens the shared view yourself.
"""
import io
import os
import shutil
import sys

